ument":20431,"endOffsetInUnit":1179,"evidenceHash":null,"evidenceSpanID":"evidence-0006","evidenceText":"For each iteration, we also saved the value of net partial P balance and calculated the total proportion (by area) of the MBB that received a PSR observation of *>*0.1, the broadly applicable M3-PSR threshold value identified by Dari et al. [\\(2018\\)](#page-10-0).","sectionID":"pub:36:sec:0020","sectionTitle":"**2.6.4 Data extrapolation, uncertainty, and visualization**","sourceArtifactID":"https://doi.org/10.1002/jeq2.20633","sourceUnitID":"pub:36:sec:0020:unit:0001","sourceUnitTextHash":"01b3a818239c2253e9cee12c67d168b29a3fdb11169d4236c4a601ca99eb7ba3","startOffsetInDocument":20167,"startOffsetInUnit":915},{"endOffsetInDocument":20543,"endOffsetInUnit":1291,"evidenceHash":null,"evidenceSpanID":"evidence-0007","evidenceText":"This approach resulted in distributions of possible results for each parameter, helping to clarify uncertainty.","sectionID":"pub:36:sec:0020","sectionTitle":"**2.6.4 Data extrapolation, uncertainty, and visualization**","sourceArtifactID":"https://doi.org/10.1002/jeq2.20633","sourceUnitID":"pub:36:sec:0020:unit:0001","sourceUnitTextHash":"01b3a818239c2253e9cee12c67d168b29a3fdb11169d4236c4a601ca99eb7ba3","startOffsetInDocument":20432,"startOffsetInUnit":1180},{"endOffsetInDocument":20905,"endOffsetInUnit":1653,"evidenceHash":null,"evidenceSpanID":"evidence-0008","evidenceText":"Lastly, we repeated iterations of the NMP → MBB extrapolation model until it produced an output with means *<* |0.1 × SD| away from the mean values of 10,000 runs for per-ha P balance and PSR, and *<* |0.25\\*SD| away from the mean values of 10,000 runs for corn cropland per-ha P balance, hay cropland per-ha P balance, corn cropland PSR, and hay cropland PSR.","sectionID":"pub:36:sec:0020","sectionTitle":"**2.6.4 Data extrapolation, uncertainty, and visualization**","sourceArtifactID":"https://doi.org/10.1002/jeq2.20633","sourceUnitID":"pub:36:sec:0020:unit:0001","sourceUnitTextHash":"01b3a818239c2253e9cee12c67d168b29a3fdb11169d4236c4a601ca99eb7ba3","startOffsetInDocument":20545,"startOffsetInUnit":1293},{"endOffsetInDocument":21086,"endOffsetInUnit":1834,"evidenceHash":null,"evidenceSpanID":"evidence-0009","evidenceText":"We refer to this result as the \"centrally trending model iteration\" and regard this model iteration as being as close to the theoretical mean basin-wide extrapolation as practical.","sectionID":"pub:36:sec:0020","sectionTitle":"**2.6.4 Data extrapolation, uncertainty, and visualization**","sourceArtifactID":"https://doi.org/10.1002/jeq2.20633","sourceUnitID":"pub:36:sec:0020:unit:0001","sourceUnitTextHash":"01b3a818239c2253e9cee12c67d168b29a3fdb11169d4236c4a601ca99eb7ba3","startOffsetInDocument":20906,"startOffsetInUnit":1654},{"endOffsetInDocument":21189,"endOffsetInUnit":1937,"evidenceHash":null,"evidenceSpanID":"evidence-0010","evidenceText":"To protect farmer privacy when displaying the prediction in GIS, we performed some minor aggregation.","sectionID":"pub:36:sec:0020","sectionTitle":"**2.6.4 Data extrapolation, uncertainty, and visualization**","sourceArtifactID":"https://doi.org/10.1002/jeq2.20633","sourceUnitID":"pub:36:sec:0020:unit:0001","sourceUnitTextHash":"01b3a818239c2253e9cee12c67d168b29a3fdb11169d4236c4a601ca99eb7ba3","startOffsetInDocument":21088,"startOffsetInUnit":1836},{"endOffsetInDocument":21379,"endOffsetInUnit":2127,"evidenceHash":null,"evidenceSpanID":"evidence-0011","evidenceText":"We computed spatially weighted mean values of P balance, PSR, and MMP for the centrally trending model iteration within each combination of land use type and NHDPlus HR HUC-12 subwatershed.","sectionID":"pub:36:sec:0020","sectionTitle":"**2.6.4 Data extrapolation, uncertainty, and visualization**","sourceArtifactID":"https://doi.org/10.1002/jeq2.20633","sourceUnitID":"pub:36:sec:0020:unit:0001","sourceUnitTextHash":"01b3a818239c2253e9cee12c67d168b29a3fdb11169d4236c4a601ca99eb7ba3","startOffsetInDocument":21190,"startOffsetInUnit":1938},{"endOffsetInDocument":21499,"endOffsetInUnit":2247,"evidenceHash":null,"evidenceSpanID":"evidence-0012","evidenceText":"A total of 25 HUC-12-level subwatersheds existed in the MBB, with 82 distinct combinations of HUC-12 and land use type.","sectionID":"pub:36:sec:0020","sectionTitle":"**2.6.4 Data extrapolation, uncertainty, and visualization**","sourceArtifactID":"https://doi.org/10.1002/jeq2.20633","sourceUnitID":"pub:36:sec:0020:unit:0001","sourceUnitTextHash":"01b3a818239c2253e9cee12c67d168b29a3fdb11169d4236c4a601ca99eb7ba3","startOffsetInDocument":21380,"startOffsetInUnit":2128},{"endOffsetInDocument":21723,"endOffsetInUnit":2471,"evidenceHash":null,"evidenceSpanID":"evidence-0013","evidenceText":"Spatially weighted means were computed by multiplying each field's P balance rate by the field's area, summing these totals within one HUC-12/land use type group, then dividing by the total area of all fields in that group.","sectionID":"pub:36:sec:0020","sectionTitle":"**2.6.4 Data extrapolation, uncertainty, and visualization**","sourceArtifactID":"https://doi.org/10.1002/jeq2.20633","sourceUnitID":"pub:36:sec:0020:unit:0001","sourceUnitTextHash":"01b3a818239c2253e9cee12c67d168b29a3fdb11169d4236c4a601ca99eb7ba3","startOffsetInDocument":21500,"startOffsetInUnit":2248},{"endOffsetInDocument":21896,"endOffsetInUnit":2644,"evidenceHash":null,"evidenceSpanID":"evidence-0014","evidenceText":"This provided smoother visualization of trends by cluster across the landscape, highlighting areas with higher or lower P parameter values, as opposed to individual fields.","sectionID":"pub:36:sec:0020","sectionTitle":"**2.6.4 Data extrapolation, uncertainty, and visualization**","sourceArtifactID":"https://doi.org/10.1002/jeq2.20633","sourceUnitID":"pub:36:sec:0020:unit:0001","sourceUnitTextHash":"01b3a818239c2253e9cee12c67d168b29a3fdb11169d4236c4a601ca99eb7ba3","startOffsetInDocument":21724,"startOffsetInUnit":2472},{"endOffsetInDocument":22149,"endOffsetInUnit":2897,"evidenceHash":null,"evidenceSpanID":"evidence-0015","evidenceText":"Second, smoothing the data for public representation reinforces that the model is an informed estimate of spatial P saturation and soil test P conditions for agricultural land types across the MBB but should not be used to make field-level conclusions.","sectionID":"pub:36:sec:0020","sectionTitle":"**2.6.4 Data extrapolation, uncertainty, and visualization**","sourceArtifactID":"https://doi.org/10.1002/jeq2.20633","sourceUnitID":"pub:36:sec:0020:unit:0001","sourceUnitTextHash":"01b3a818239c2253e9cee12c67d168b29a3fdb11169d4236c4a601ca99eb7ba3","startOffsetInDocument":21897,"startOffsetInUnit":2645}]},"evaluationStatus":"NOT_FORMAL_EVALUATION","goldStatus":"NOT_GOLD","modelCallMade":false,"providerCalls":0,"transportMethod":"COUNTERFACTUAL_TRANSPORT_EMULATION"}
